# Echo

Echo is a mimic bot the idea is for it to play chess like me (chess.com user `abzalya`, ~1500 rapid).

To achieve this, Echo's approach is two parted:

1. **Opening book** — built from ~8,200 (to-date) chess.com games. Each position is
   hashed and moves recorded with frequency. Echo samples moves
   weighted by how often that move was actually played, so it reproduces my repertoire.

2. **Maia** — once the book runs out (typically after 8-15 plies), moves are handed off to
   Maia, a neural network trained to predict what a human at a given Elo would play. It runs
   inside lc0 at `go nodes 1`, one policy evaluation, no search. Which preserves the human-mimicking property. For additional context on Maia, see:
   - Maia paper (for context, not required reading): https://arxiv.org/abs/2006.01855
    - Maia GitHub: https://github.com/CSSLab/maia-chess

Echo is a UCI engine. Run it in CuteChess or any UCI-compatible GUI.

Unlike Nomad, Echo is not self-contained out of the box. You need to build the book and supply the lc0 binary and Maia weights. They have been gitignored.

---

## Setup

### 1. Build the book

Run these two scripts in order from the repo root:

```
python echo/fetcher.py
python echo/book_builder.py
```

`fetcher.py` pulls chess.com games into `echo/artifacts/games_raw.pgn`.
`book_builder.py` parses them and writes `echo/artifacts/echo_book.pkl`.

To inspect the book afterwards:

```
python echo/inspect_book.py stats
python echo/inspect_book.py lookup --after "e2e4"
```

### 3. Download lc0

Echo uses lc0 to run Maia. Download the right binary for your machine:

**Windows (modern CPU)**
Download `lc0-vX.XX.X-windows-cpu-dnnl.zip` from
`https://github.com/LeelaChessZero/lc0/releases/latest`
Copy `lc0.exe`, `dnnl.dll`, `mimalloc-override.dll`, `mimalloc-redirect.dll` into `echo/bin/`.

**macOS (Apple Silicon / modern Intel)**
Download `lc0-vX.XX.X-macos.tar.gz` from the same releases page.
Extract and copy the `lc0` binary into `echo/bin/`.
You may need to run `xattr -d com.apple.quarantine echo/bin/lc0` to clear Gatekeeper.

**Linux (older CPU, no AVX2 — e.g. 2013 Mac Pro running Linux)**
Download the BLAS build: `lc0-vX.XX.X-linux-blas.tar.gz`.
The standard builds assume AVX2; the BLAS build does not. Copy the `lc0` binary into `echo/bin/`.

### 4. Download Maia weights

Download the Maia weight files from `https://github.com/CSSLab/maia-chess/releases`.

Files are named `maia-1100.pb.gz` through `maia-1900.pb.gz` (one per Elo bucket).
Place them in `echo/artifacts/weights/`.

The default is `maia-1500.pb.gz`. Download at least that one.

### 5. Sanity check lc0

Before running Echo, verify lc0 works with the weights. From the repo root:

```
.\echo\bin\lc0.exe --weights=echo\artifacts\weights\maia-1500.pb.gz   # Windows
./echo/bin/lc0     --weights=echo/artifacts/weights/maia-1500.pb.gz   # Linux / macOS
```

Then type manually:
```
uci
position startpos
go nodes 1
```

If you get `bestmove <move>` back, you are good.

---

## Running in CuteChess

Use `echo/echo.bat` (Windows) as the engine command in CuteChess:

```
C:\path\to\chess-and-engine\echo\echo.bat
```

The `.bat` file sets the working directory and Python path correctly before launching `uci.py`.

On Linux / macOS, point CuteChess at a shell script equivalent:
```bash
#!/bin/bash
cd /path/to/chess-and-engine
python -m echo.uci
```

---

## UCI options

| Option   | Type  | Default | Effect                                          |
|----------|-------|---------|-------------------------------------------------|
| UseBook  | check | true    | Toggle the opening book on/off                  |
| BookOnly | check | false   | Never call Maia; play random moves out of book  |
| MaiaElo  | combo | 1500    | Which Maia weight file to use (1100–1900)       |
